eurlex/decider/jev:
{
  "model": "jev-latest",
  "state": "<Insert the original EUR-Lex input, including all four marked blocks>",
  "questions": {
    "mode": {
      "type": "choice",
      "instructions": {
        "task": "Select the best generation mode for the TARGET ARTICLE; do not generate questions.\nThe input contains TARGET ARTICLE, REFERENCED ARTICLES, REFERENCED ARTICLES FROM OTHER ACTS and REFERENCED ANNEXES. The target supplies the operative rule; supplied references may complete it. Metadata identifies the regime, not an answer. A missing reference excludes only questions depending on it.\nFACT_PATTERN: a natural third-person case involving a private party or national authority handling a file, with at least two meaningful particulars establishing scope or conditions. Actor, product/activity, conduct, territory, amount and timing can count; arbitrary names, places and numbers cannot. Each fact need not change a numerical answer. The case asks how the explicit rule treats it without stating the consequence. Matching stipulated facts to explicit categories, conditions, thresholds or dates is allowed. No extra dispute or dilemma is required. Category-only questions do not require unrelated conditions for ultimate entitlement.\nLOOKUP: a direct regime-anchored request for a definition, scope, obligation, permission, condition, deadline, amount, reporting duty, consequence, body or procedure. No scenario is needed. Select it when no natural permitted case with two meaningful particulars is supported; do not inflate scenarios to fill two slots.\nTIE POLICY: if both modes support good questions and the case passes the stated test, choose fact_pattern. A possible abstract rewrite does not disqualify it. No mode quotas.\nBoth modes need identifier-free wording that identifies the regime. Use the strongest substantive opportunity; one good question suffices. Skip when neither mode supports a substantive, grounded question, including boilerplate-only transposition, application/entry into force, addressees, binding-entirety, repeal, committee procedure, definitive collection, institutional review/report/evaluation, financial-interests/audit access, delegation, generic penalties and data-protection cross-references. Substantive rules in mixed articles remain usable. The Commission, EU agencies/officials and Member States acting as legislators are not case protagonists; non-excluded institutional duties may support lookup.\n"
      },
      "criteria": {
        "fact_pattern": "A concrete case involving a private party or a national authority handling a file, without naming the governing instrument. At least two meaningful particulars establish scope or conditions of the target rule. It asks how an explicit rule treats that case: applicability, permission, obligation, prerequisite, deadline, amount, procedure or consequence. Ordinary condition/category/threshold/date matching is allowed; unsupported law or missing decisive facts are not. A routine case is sufficient; no added dispute or dilemma is needed. If both modes are viable, apply the stated tie policy.",
        "lookup": "A direct request for a definition, scope rule, obligation, condition, deadline, threshold, reporting duty, consequence, competent body or procedure with a specific regime anchor. Two renderings differ only by the target act identifier. Select this when no natural permitted case with two meaningful particulars is supported; an arbitrary actor or irrelevant detail does not make a case.",
        "skip": "Neither mode supports a substantive, informative, grounded question about the target. This includes boilerplate such as transposition, general entry into force, repeal, generic institutional review or generic penalties, and cases where indispensable answer substance is missing."
      }
    }
  }
}

un/decider/jev:
{
  "model": "jev-latest",
  "state": "<Insert the original UN input, including all three marked sections>",
  "questions": {
    "mode": {
      "type": "choice",
      "instructions": {
        "task": "Select lookup, practitioner, semantic or skip for a UN TARGET BLOCK. Supporting context/references/metadata resolve identity, time and attribution, never missing answers. All document types including PV/SR support every mode.\nLookup: a precise fact when a particular source is naturally part of the information need; render source description and identifier versions.\nPractitioner: a precise specialist need identified by regime/situation, attribution and time without a target-document locator. Requests, proposals and reported findings qualify with their status preserved; binding law is not required.\nSemantic: a conceptual situation, response, rationale or stakeholder need, supported directly by the text; not an isolated datum disguised as how/why.\nSkip: no specific informative answer is available under any mode.\nConsider the strongest plausible question in each mode, then choose the most useful natural fit. Do not output those questions. Source identifiers, option order, conceptual breadth, expected grades or desired frequencies do not decide. Lookup versus practitioner depends on source-seeking versus situation-seeking intent; neither has priority. Require adequate substantive identification and relevant absolute time equally, not a fixed anchor count. One distinctive composite anchor may suffice. Recurring or non-binding content can be useful; do not demand yearly novelty or invent duplicates. Judge propositions, not ceremonial verbs in mixed passages. Preserve actual speaker, attribution, and request/proposal/authorization status. One useful question is enough; skip only when every mode fails.\n"
      },
      "criteria": {
        "lookup": "A precise need whose natural formulation locates a point in a particular source; description and identifier renderings preserve subject, attribution and time.",
        "practitioner": "A precise specialist norm, task, proposal, procedure or reported fact naturally identified by regime/situation and relevant time without a target-document locator.",
        "semantic": "A bounded conceptual situation, response, rationale or stakeholder need, directly supported by the target; not an isolated datum or vague speech report.",
        "skip": "No specific informative question is supported in any mode; ceremony or missing substance may justify skip, but document type, recurrence or non-binding status alone do not."
      }
    }
  }
}
